Cystic Fibrosis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Meconium ileus, Recurrent respiratory infections, Steatorrhea/Failure to thrive

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-016
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of chronic wet cough, new-onset tachypnea, and increased work of breathing requiring urgent evaluation for acute respiratory failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly increasing respiratory rate and new-onset lethargy in a child with known recurrent respiratory infections and failure to thrive. Immediate assessment was mandated to evaluate oxygenation status, rule out acute lobar pneumonia, and prevent imminent respiratory decompensation prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They are coughing up thick green mucous constantly today and they look absolutely exhausted trying to breathe."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a persistent, daily, wet, and highly productive cough that has acutely worsened over the last 24 hours, now generating copious thick, tenacious, purulent sputum. The caregivers report a linear progression starting with mild nasal congestion two days ago, rapidly evolving into severe coughing spasms and lethargy today. The patient has a known history of recurrent respiratory infections and pronounced failure to thrive due to steatorrhea, despite a voracious appetite. The caregivers report the child's skin tastes excessively salty.
Functional Status: Fatigued, unable to complete full sentences without coughing.
Social/Safety Concerns: High caregiver anxiety regarding the rapid respiratory decline and poor weight gain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 125 | Temp: 37.8C | RR: 32 | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 14 Kg
General: Thin, pale, tachypneic, visibly using accessory muscles to breathe.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals intercostal retractions and a prolonged expiratory phase. Auscultation demonstrates widespread coarse bilateral crackles and expiratory wheeze. Digital clubbing is clearly evident on the hands, reflecting chronic hypoxia. The abdomen is slightly distended but soft, non-tender, with hyperactive bowel sounds. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed historically and clinically.
Environmental Observations: Nebulizer machine on the bedside table; the room is excessively warm.
Medication Reconciliation: Pancreatic enzymes administered with dinner; compliance confirmed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cystic Fibrosis - Status: Acute Respiratory Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out due to the presence of thick, purulent sputum, coarse crackles rather than isolated wheeze, and digital clubbing.

Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia - Unlikely due to the prominent presence of severe steatorrhea and failure to thrive, which strongly points to pancreatic exocrine insufficiency.

Pertussis - Ruled out due to the chronicity of the underlying wet cough, the presence of clubbing, and the absence of a characteristic inspiratory whoop.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of worsening tachypnea, purulent sputum production, and borderline hypoxia necessitated an urgent after-hours physical assessment. Telephone triage recognized the high vulnerability of this patient to rapid respiratory decompensation requiring immediate auscultation and pulse oximetry. Objective evaluation confirmed an acute lower respiratory tract exacerbation superimposed on chronic lung disease, with an SpO2 of 92%. Delaying care until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptable risk of profound hypoxic respiratory failure and septicemia. Immediate intervention permitted the initiation of aggressive chest physiotherapy and targeted oral antibiotics, safely stabilizing the patient in the community while arranging urgent specialist follow-up.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate. Directed immediate administration of a hypertonic saline nebulizer.
Positive Behavioral Change: During chest physiotherapy, provide a high-value physical reward (e.g., a sticker or extra screen time) immediately after the child successfully clears and expectorates a sputum plug into a tissue. This directly reinforces the difficult and uncomfortable physical effort required for effective airway clearance, establishing a positive feedback loop for compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent notification to the pediatric respiratory team.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 12-24 hours to reassess oxygenation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes cyanotic around the lips, exhibits severe indrawing of the chest wall, or becomes completely unresponsive.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis and acute exacerbation severity require close, dynamic monitoring.
Clinical Course: Expect the cough to remain highly productive over the next 3-5 days as the antibiotics and physiotherapy mobilize the infected secretions.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the child struggles for every breath, turns blue, or cannot be woken up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The thick mucous in the lungs has become infected, requiring immediate antibiotics. The nebulizer and chest clapping are critical to physically dislodge this mucous so it can be coughed out.
Actionable Steps: Perform chest physiotherapy strictly every 4 hours tonight. Ensure the child drinks plenty of water to help thin the secretions.

